# Getting Started, part 4: starting, setting up and a first document.
.h1 Starting Strata [starting]
.h2 The Document Manager [manager]
Start the Document Manager, in its terminal version or in its window version.
After an install under {c:/usr}, the terminal version is started with:
.code
/usr/bin/str
.end
and the window version with:
.code
/usr/bin/str-x11
.end
The menu lists the things you do with documents.
.pic pictures/str-menu.png 5.5 The Document Manager. [menu-figure]
Move with {k:Up} and {k:Down}, or press the first letter of an entry, and press
{k:Ins} or {k:Enter} to choose it.  {k:Esc} goes back and {k:F1} shows the help
for the entry.  The selections are shown in {fig:menu-figure}.
{b:Edit / Preview / Print Document} opens a panel with the
{b:Working Directory}, the {b:Document Name} and {b:Variables}; the cursor is
on the document name.  Type the name and press {k:Ins}, and the Editor opens
the document.  A name that does not exist leads to the question whether the new
document is to be {b:Structured} or {b:Unstructured}, and then to a panel for a
description and an author, which may stay empty.  Printing, previewing and the
spell check are done in the Editor.  {b:Maintain Dictionaries} keeps the words
of your own, the words that the spell check must not report; the dictionary of
the language itself comes from the spelling program of the system, and is not
managed by Strata.
{b:Compare Document Versions} shows what changed between a document and an older
copy of it.  {b:TeX Font Association} and {b:Setup} are explained below.
.h3 Names of documents [names]
A document name is an ordinary file name of your system.  Use names that say
what the document is, such as {c:2026-budget-proposal.doc} or
{c:filing-system-memo.doc}.  The directory and the name may be as long as the
system allows.
.h3 Templates [templates]
When the name does not exist, a list shows the templates that Strata comes with,
as well as the two kinds of documents: letters in the style of the LaTeX letter
class for US Letter and for A4 paper, envelopes for many countries and the
label sheets of Avery.  The templates are in subdirectories (letters,
envelopes and labels): Enter on a subdirectory opens it, and Left goes up.  Each template has a
description, which is shown below the list when the template is chosen.
.pic pictures/file-new-list.png 5.5 The list of the templates, with the description of the one that is chosen. [new-list-figure]
{Fig:new-list-figure} shows the list.  The description is a line of text that
you can write for any document of your own, in the panel of New File or Save
File.
.h2 The Editor [editor]
You open a document from the Document Manager: choose Edit / Preview / Print
Document and give the name of a document, such as {c:memo.doc}.  The screen has
a status line at the top, the text in the middle, and the function keys at the
bottom.
.pic pictures/stred-memo.png 5.5 A document in the Editor.
The status line shows the name of the document (with a star in front of it when
it has changes that are not saved), the attribute of the text under the cursor,
the position, the number of the section, and the mode: {c:STRUCTURED EDIT} for a
document with sections, {c:UNSTRUCTURED EDIT} for one without.  The two lines at
the foot name the function keys {k:F1} to {k:F12}.  Press {k:F12} for the menu
and {k:F1} for help; {k:Ctrl-S} saves and {k:Ctrl-Q} leaves the Editor.  If you
make a mistake, {k:Ctrl-/} takes back the last change and {k:Alt-/} makes it
again.
.h2 Terminal or window [terminal-or-x11]
In a terminal, some keys never reach the program, because the terminal or the
window manager keeps them; {x:keys-conflict} lists them.  Strata has
other keys for those: {k:Ctrl-F} opens a menu of the {k:Alt-F} commands,
{k:Ctrl-F12} opens the marker commands and {k:Alt-O} switches overtype.  In the
window every key arrives as it is, unless the window manager keeps it.  The
keypad keys {k:Keypad +}, {k:Keypad -} and {k:Keypad *} fold and open sections.
Equations, keycaps and pictures are shown as images only in the window.
In the window the wheel of the mouse scrolls the text and a click puts the
cursor where the arrow is ({x:appx11-mouse}); every command is also on a
key, and the terminal version does not use the mouse.
The window is 80 columns wide and 50 lines high until you change it.  The height
is the setting Window Rows of Setup, or you can change the height of the window
with your window manager: the height that you give the window is kept.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
The environment variables {c:STRATA_X11_GEOM} and {c:STRATA_X11_SIZE} change it.
.endif
.h2 Pictures keep their colours [colour-pictures]
A picture that has colour is printed in colour.  A screenshot, a chart, a
drawing or a photograph that you put into a document with a Picture marker keeps
every colour that its file has, and a colour printer prints it in colour, in a
draft and in a final print, whether the file is PNG, JPEG, PDF or EPS.  Only the
field {b:Print in Black and White?} of the print panels turns the pictures into
greys.  The window version of the Editor shows a picture in colour in the text
too; a terminal cannot draw pictures at all.
.pic pictures/print-colour.png 5.5 A picture with colour, as it is printed.

.h1 Setting up Strata [setup]
.h2 The Setup panel [setup-panel]
In the Document Manager choose {b:Setup}.
.pic pictures/str-setup.png 5.5 The Setup panel of the Document Manager.
The fields stand in four groups.  {b:Documents}: {b:Initial Working Directory},
the directory that panels start in, {b:Backups Kept}, {b:Autosave} and its
interval.  {b:Printing}: {b:Print To} and {b:Printer Queue}, where a PDF or a
print is sent, the {b:PDF Directory} and the {b:PDF Viewer}.  {b:Writing}:
{b:Paper for New Documents}, {b:Dictionary Language} and {b:Undo Levels}.
{b:Screen}: {b:Window Rows}.  Press {k:PgUp} for the next page, the colors of
the screen, and again for the third page, the advanced settings that are seldom
changed ({k:PgDn} goes back): {b:Initial Macro Set}, {b:Initial Library},
{b:Terminal Characters} (VT100 unless you change it), the {b:Editor program} that {b:Edit / Preview / Print
Document} starts and the {b:Help File}.  The default of a field is shown at its
right, and {k:Del} puts it back.  A field that holds a name of a directory or a
file lists the choices when you press {k:Keypad +}.  A wrong value is refused in
its field, with the reason at the foot of the panel, and nothing else that you
typed is lost.  Press {k:Ins} to keep the changes.
.h2 The colors [colors]
The screen is yellow on blue by default.  In {b:Setup} press {k:PgUp}: the
second page lists the kinds of text, such as normal, bold, the menus and the
status line, and lets you choose a foreground and a background for each,
separately for the terminal and for the window; {k:Left}, {k:Right} and
{k:Space} choose the color and {k:F2} shows the palette.  {k:Ins} keeps the
colors and {k:Esc} leaves them as they were.
.h2 Where the settings are [settings]
The settings are kept in the directory {c:${{HOME}}/.strata}.
The file {c:config} holds the settings of the Setup panel and the file
{c:fonts} holds the font list below.  The environment variable {c:STRATA_HOME}
names another directory, for a second set of settings.
.h2 The fonts [fonts]
A printed document names its fonts by number, from 1 to 20.  The list that says
which LaTeX font each number is, is the {b:TeX Font Association}.
.pic pictures/str-fonts.png 5.5 The fonts of the TeX Font Association.
It starts with the standard fonts of LaTeX, such as Latin Modern, Times,
Helvetica, Palatino and TeX Gyre, and a font that is not installed is marked
as such.  A
font that is free can be downloaded from the network of TeX archives with
{b:Install Now}, if {c:curl} and {c:unzip} are installed; you can also add your
own.
.h2 The help [help]
{k:F1} shows help.  The topic depends on where you are: in a panel the help is
about the field, in the text about the command that you used last.  The help
opens in a window: {k:Tab} moves to the next link and {k:Enter} follows it,
{k:Backspace} goes back, {k:I} shows the index and {k:Esc} closes it.  The text
comes from a help file, which the Setup panel names.

.h1 Your first document [first]
Here is a short walk, to show that it works. The Tutorial that follows takes
you through each step in turn.
1. Start the Document Manager, choose {b:Edit / Preview / Print Document}, type
   {c:hello.doc} and press {k:Ins}.  The document does not exist, so choose
   {b:Structured}, and press {k:Ins} on the panel that asks for a description
   and an author.
2. Type a headline, press {k:Enter}, and type a sentence.
3. Press {k:Ctrl-S}.  The document is saved with the name {c:hello.doc}.
4. Press {k:F12}, choose {b:Print}, then {b:Preview}, then {b:Document}.  Press
   {k:Ins} on the panel.  After a moment the pages appear in a window; press
   {k:q} to close it.
5. Press {k:Ctrl-Q} to leave the Editor.

If something does not work, the chapter {x:need} says what each step needs;
Preview, for example, needs LaTeX and an X display.
